1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,663
(upbeat music)

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,080
- [Instructor] Let us now
discuss the very important notion

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:15,080
or concept of indifference
set or indifference curve.

4
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:18,540
An indifference set is a set of bundles

5
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:22,710
that the consumer regard as equal, okay?

6
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:25,470
So it's a set of bundles

7
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:29,490
that the consumer regards as equal.

8
00:00:29,490 --> 00:00:34,490
What that means is that
inside the elements

9
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:39,420
of an indifference set are bundles

10
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:43,650
such that for the corresponding consumer,

11
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:46,530
all those bundles feel the same.

12
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:50,403
They are indifferent
with each other, okay?

13
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:55,710
For example, suppose
we have a bundle, okay?

14
00:00:55,710 --> 00:01:00,690
And then we collect or identify

15
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:03,130
all other bundles that

16
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:08,860
are indifferent to x

17
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,630
for this particular consumer,

18
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:15,270
and that whole set will be

19
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:18,570
the indifference curve that contains x,

20
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:23,493
and we will denote it
by the symbol I of x.

21
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,453
Remember, by definition,

22
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:32,820
all the bundles in this
set can have this property

23
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:34,473
or will have this property.

24
00:01:36,119 --> 00:01:39,257
That will be that each of them, sorry,

25
00:01:41,730 --> 00:01:44,313
each of them will be indifferent to x.

26
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:48,510
However, it is important to note

27
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:53,373
that an indifference set
is not necessarily a curve.

28
00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:57,803
An indifference set could be a band

29
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:03,960
or a set that is thick, as
we will see in some examples.

30
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:06,090
Under some assumptions, however,

31
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:09,660
this indifference set
will indeed become curves,

32
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:12,153
meaning they will be infinitely thin.

33
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:20,133
This is an example of
an indifference curve.

34
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,870
Suppose we have bundle 3, 4,

35
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:28,140
where again x-axis is coffee

36
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:32,460
and y-axis is orange juice.

37
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:36,780
So in this, by this statement here,

38
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:40,560
we're saying that the
bundle 3 ounces of coffee

39
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:44,550
and 4 cups of orange juice is indifferent

40
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:47,460
to the bundle of 1 ounce coffee

41
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:50,910
and 12 cups of orange juice, okay?

42
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:53,850
So if that is correct,

43
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:58,740
if that statement is indeed
true for this consumer,

44
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:02,880
that means that the same
set or, in this case,

45
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:07,080
the same curve contains both 3, 4

46
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:07,980
and 1, 12.

47
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:12,060
In this example, we are also
denoting that the bundle 6, 2

48
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:14,403
is also in the same indifference set.

49
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,400
There is a notion that is also important

50
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,443
of the weakly preferred set,

51
00:03:23,370 --> 00:03:26,610
which is the area that is preferred

52
00:03:26,610 --> 00:03:29,640
to a whole indifference curve

53
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:33,120
or some particular bundle.

54
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:36,500
In this case, the shaded area here

55
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:38,520
is the weakly preferred set

56
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:41,880
to this indifference curve denoted by IC,

57
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:45,450
which is indifference curve
associated with bundles 3, 4,

58
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:47,973
1, 12, 6, 2, and many other.

59
00:03:50,430 --> 00:03:52,470
Another very interesting question is

60
00:03:52,470 --> 00:03:56,643
whether we have one or
many indifference curves.

61
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:01,623
In the commodity space, we
typically have infinite.

62
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:05,850
In fact, we will, in most cases,

63
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:10,533
have uncountable, infinite
indifference curves.

64
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:15,330
However, we do not need to draw, you know,

65
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:18,690
infinite or not even
many indifference curves.

66
00:04:18,690 --> 00:04:23,690
Usually to describe the
consumer preferences

67
00:04:24,270 --> 00:04:28,560
or the corresponding consumer preferences,

68
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,760
we only need to draw
few indifference curves.

69
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,330
The shape of these indifference curves

70
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:41,330
is in particular the slope
and how curvy they are.

71
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:45,060
Those are giving or providing information

72
00:04:45,060 --> 00:04:47,490
about these preferences.

73
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:49,050
In this particular example here,

74
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:51,810
we have a case in which we have drawn

75
00:04:51,810 --> 00:04:54,120
only four indifference curves,

76
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:57,690
and we can see that this consumer

77
00:04:57,690 --> 00:05:02,640
has monotonic preferences

78
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:06,030
because indifference
curves are downward sloped,

79
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:10,420
and we more or less can
say that this consumer

80
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:16,820
has similar preferences for Good 1 and 2.

81
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:20,823
We will see other examples in
which that is not the case.

82
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:28,320
So other or another set of
concepts that is very important

83
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:32,520
is that of commodities being good,

84
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:35,040
bad or neutral, okay?

85
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:38,400
So a good commodity is the one

86
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:41,430
that increases the satisfaction

87
00:05:41,430 --> 00:05:45,330
or it is better for the consumer

88
00:05:45,330 --> 00:05:47,730
to have more of it, right?

89
00:05:47,730 --> 00:05:50,520
So that's a good, so for example,

90
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:55,520
if I'm eating one ounce of chocolate

91
00:05:56,970 --> 00:06:01,890
and eating more chocolate
will make me happier,

92
00:06:01,890 --> 00:06:03,630
I would prefer to eat more chocolate.

93
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:06,030
Then at that point,

94
00:06:06,030 --> 00:06:09,540
chocolate will be a good commodity for me.

95
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:11,220
It will increase my satisfaction.

96
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:14,190
I would prefer to have more of it,

97
00:06:14,190 --> 00:06:15,810
but there are other ones that,

98
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:18,843
there are other commodities
that could become a bad.

99
00:06:19,830 --> 00:06:23,880
So for example, trash is a
bad in the sense that I want,

100
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:25,710
I do not want more of it.

101
00:06:25,710 --> 00:06:27,900
I want less of it.

102
00:06:27,900 --> 00:06:32,900
So trash is an example of a bad commodity,

103
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:39,333
not necessarily something
needs to be bad like all along.

104
00:06:43,078 --> 00:06:43,911
For example, I could regard chocolate good

105
00:06:47,550 --> 00:06:51,150
to a certain point but then
could become a bad to me.

106
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:52,980
So maybe I can eat all the way

107
00:06:52,980 --> 00:06:56,280
to half a kilogram of chocolate,

108
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:59,910
and then if I eat more
than that in a week,

109
00:06:59,910 --> 00:07:02,610
let's say a week is the time horizon,

110
00:07:02,610 --> 00:07:06,243
then more of it will actually hurt me.

111
00:07:07,470 --> 00:07:09,390
I will not like to have more

112
00:07:09,390 --> 00:07:12,390
after I have eaten
already half a kilogram.

113
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:14,310
Maybe I would like to have less.

114
00:07:14,310 --> 00:07:17,550
In that sense, after half a kilogram,

115
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,443
chocolate will become a bad,

116
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:23,520
and the next concept

117
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:26,190
is the concept of neutrality.

118
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:31,190
So a commodity is neutral if I don't care

119
00:07:31,470 --> 00:07:34,920
if I consume more or less of it.

120
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:35,940
I just don't care.

121
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:38,610
It doesn't increase nor
decrease my satisfaction

122
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:41,103
to have more or less of that.

123
00:07:45,030 --> 00:07:48,333
There is a fourth assumption
that is called convexity.

124
00:07:49,260 --> 00:07:53,790
Convexity is the assumption
related to how people,

125
00:07:53,790 --> 00:07:58,230
humans prefer to consume
some form of balance,

126
00:07:58,230 --> 00:08:02,940
balanced bundles, in the sense
that if I have two goods,

127
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:06,093
in my example, I was using
coffee and orange juice,

128
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:12,090
in average a consumer
or a typical consumer

129
00:08:12,090 --> 00:08:15,570
would not like to drink only
coffee or only orange juice

130
00:08:15,570 --> 00:08:17,950
but would prefer to have some combination

131
00:08:18,810 --> 00:08:21,480
of these two goods, okay?

132
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,040
So one example would be that

133
00:08:25,410 --> 00:08:30,410
a 50-50 mixture of two bundles,

134
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:34,023
one bundle having only
coffee and another bundle,

135
00:08:35,213 --> 00:08:37,503
y, having only orange juice,

136
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:42,280
a 50-50 bundle that combines

137
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:49,050
the average amount of coffee

138
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:50,850
between those two bundles, x and y,

139
00:08:50,850 --> 00:08:54,510
and the average amount of orange juice

140
00:08:54,510 --> 00:08:58,140
between those two bundles
could be preferred

141
00:08:58,140 --> 00:08:59,880
to either extreme.

142
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:04,060
If that is true, then this consumer

143
00:09:05,430 --> 00:09:08,310
obeys this assumption or
follows this assumption

144
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:11,160
of convexity, okay?

145
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:14,700
That is, mixture bundles
are weakly preferred

146
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:16,773
to the extreme bundles.

147
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:24,443
Let's see how graphically convexity shapes

148
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:31,520
or gives form to the preferences
and indifference curves.

149
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:34,710
So again,

150
00:09:34,710 --> 00:09:38,190
we will have two goods,

151
00:09:38,190 --> 00:09:41,193
and we will have, you
know, this will be coffee,

152
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:46,620
and this will have orange juice,

153
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:51,510
and we're saying that we have two bundles.

154
00:09:51,510 --> 00:09:53,910
One has only orange juice,

155
00:09:53,910 --> 00:09:58,590
and the other one has only coffee,

156
00:09:58,590 --> 00:10:03,590
and convexity says that
a more balanced bundle,

157
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:06,660
c, that has kind of an average amount

158
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:08,910
of coffee between those two bundles,

159
00:10:08,910 --> 00:10:12,570
an average amount of orange
juice between those two bundles

160
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:17,570
will be preferred to a
or and preferred to c.

161
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:21,630
So because of that, here
is the important part,

162
00:10:21,630 --> 00:10:24,450
because of that, indifference curves

163
00:10:24,450 --> 00:10:27,100
now can be written or can be

164
00:10:31,530 --> 00:10:33,513
drawn in this way.

165
00:10:43,230 --> 00:10:44,370
Why is that?

166
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:46,350
Because for this consumer

167
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:50,640
to prefer z over a and z over b,

168
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:54,120
the indifference curve
might have this shape.

169
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:58,290
If they do not have this
shape, this convex shape,

170
00:10:58,290 --> 00:11:02,700
then it wouldn't be true
that c is preferred over a

171
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:04,593
and c is preferred over b.

172
00:11:09,120 --> 00:11:14,120
Of course, not all
preferences of a on convexity.

173
00:11:14,310 --> 00:11:16,950
The plot that you see and
the figure you're seeing

174
00:11:16,950 --> 00:11:18,540
on your screen right now,

175
00:11:18,540 --> 00:11:22,680
where the darker blue curve
is an indifference curve

176
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:26,700
and the lighter blue shaded area

177
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:29,580
is a weakly preferred set,

178
00:11:29,580 --> 00:11:34,323
is such that if the two extreme bundles,

179
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:39,750
one, or sorry, x and y

180
00:11:39,750 --> 00:11:44,100
are on the same indifference set,

181
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:49,100
the average one, what in our
previous example was bundle c,

182
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:53,910
the one here that has the
name of averaged bundle,

183
00:11:53,910 --> 00:11:57,900
that one is not preferred, okay?

184
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:02,900
So for that bundle not to be preferred,

185
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:06,450
the indifference curves must look like

186
00:12:06,450 --> 00:12:10,410
this darker blue curve,

187
00:12:10,410 --> 00:12:13,440
and as you can see, the
only way that could happen

188
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:17,373
is if the preferences are not convex,

189
00:12:19,006 --> 00:12:21,208
do not satisfy convexity.

190
00:12:21,208 --> 00:12:23,791
(gentle music)

